<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  com.claude.permission-harvest — the WEEKLY cron for bin/cc-permission-harvest.

  The harvester is a read-only reporter over the permission archive, the validate-bash corpus and
  the settings fleet. Nothing scheduled it, so nothing ever asked those stores what they held:
  3,728 Bash permission prompts and 1,622 h of waiting accrued unexamined. This plist is the
  missing cron and nothing else — every gate and refusal code stays in the tool, and the APPLY is
  never a launchd side effect (the job files one cc-do-able operator step; hooks/validate-bash.sh
  denies `--apply` from inside a session at the chokepoint).

  Sunday 04:17, not on the hour and not at 04:15. 04:15 belongs to com.claude.worktree-gc-infra
  and 03:15 to reso's reaper; both walk the same filesystem this one reads. Two minutes of offset
  costs nothing and keeps a weekly reporter out of a nightly deleter's window.

  RunAtLoad FALSE — corrected 2026-09-09, and the sentence it replaces is worth keeping as the
  lesson. It read: "This one removes nothing: it reads three stores and appends one JSON line, so
  seeding the evidence at bootstrap is pure gain." The cost claim was false by three orders of
  magnitude. MEASURED under the interpreter this job actually uses (/usr/bin/python3, 30-day
  window, live stores): `real 1045.06` — 17m25s, ~920 s of it I/O wait across 3,231 archive files,
  286,466 corpus records, a depth-5 walk of ~/Development (229 s in discover_settings alone, 222
  worktree entries) and ~6,281 transcript .jsonl files / 8.5 GB. That is not "pure gain" at a
  login; it is a multi-GB scan fired every time the agent bootstraps, holding the wrapper's mkdir
  lock while it runs — and a contender that finds the lock held writes NO evidence row, so a slow
  bootstrap run also blanks the sensor cc-fleet reads.

  The cost of RunAtLoad false is that a freshly-installed job reads NEVER-RAN (cc-fleet S3: runs=0
  AND no evidence file) until the first Sunday 04:17. That is an honest state and a bounded one;
  a silently-stalled weekly job is neither. `launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/<label>` seeds it
  on demand if a reader wants the row sooner. The tool itself is now bounded too
  (CC_PERMHARVEST_MAX_S, default 3600 s — ~3.4x the 1,045 s measurement, because a bound BELOW the
  real runtime is a scheduled failure rather than a bound ⇒ exit 6 ⇒ the wrapper's `verdict=error`),
  so a hang can no longer sit on the lock indefinitely.

  `bash -c` (not a bare ProgramArguments path) because launchd expands neither ~ nor $HOME inside
  ProgramArguments. Standard*Path cannot take $HOME at all, hence the literals. The script hardens
  its own PATH on line 1 and resolves its interpreter absolutely (/usr/bin/python3 — launchd's PATH
  and the interactive PATH disagree about which python3 `python3` means).

  NO ProcessType AND NO Nice. `ProcessType Background` applies Darwin's darwinbg TASK ROLE, a
  one-way floor: every descendant sits at PRI 4, E-core confined, and `taskpolicy -c utility` from
  inside cannot climb back out (migrations/0016; tests/postland-band-floor.bats iii). The WRAPPER
  re-execs itself through /usr/sbin/taskpolicy -c utility instead — below interactive, still
  P-core eligible — and fails open when taskpolicy(8) is absent.

  Activation is the manifest's generic arm, not a bespoke script: install.sh's LaunchAgents block
  copies every launchd/*.plist into ~/Library/LaunchAgents and `bootstrap`s any label the landed
  fleet.manifest declares `run`, on every deploy-live converge. 18-fleet-activate.sh is the
  recovery arm for the NOT-INSTALLED / DISABLED states.

  Kill switch (no unload needed): CC_PERMHARVEST_BIN=/nonexistent, or retire the manifest row.
  Full stop: launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.claude.permission-harvest
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.claude.permission-harvest</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>-c</string>
        <string>exec "$HOME/.claude/scripts/permission-harvest-run.sh"</string>
    </array>

    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
    <dict>
        <key>Weekday</key>
        <integer>0</integer>
        <key>Hour</key>
        <integer>4</integer>
        <key>Minute</key>
        <integer>17</integer>
    </dict>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/chrisren/.claude/logs/permission-harvest.out.log</string>

    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/chrisren/.claude/logs/permission-harvest.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
